---

When Nx runs the app's server for your end-to-end (e2e) tests, each
[atomized](/docs/kb/split-e2e-tasks) e2e task, such as `e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts`, depends on
that continuous task.
Nx adds the [inputs of continuous dependencies](/docs/reference/inputs#inputs-of-continuous-dependencies)
to the hash of the tasks they serve, so each of these tasks hashes the whole app, and a change to
any feature reruns every e2e test:

![Without the directive, checkout.spec.ts and search.spec.ts both hash checkout/, search/, cart/, and shared/, so an edit in search/ reruns both tests.](../../../assets/guides/running-tasks/e2e-hash-whole-app.svg)

With [Ultracache](/docs/reference/project-configuration#ultracache), Nx hashes a task from the files
it read on a recorded run instead of its declared file inputs.
A test file that imports the app code it exercises makes the test runner read that code, so the
recording covers it.
Add `// @nx-ultracache: imports` to the top of the test file, and the servers' inputs stay out of
the hash of its atomized task.
The task's hash then covers the app code that the test file imports directly or through other
imports:

![With the directive, checkout.spec.ts hashes checkout/ and shared/, and search.spec.ts hashes search/ and shared/, so an edit in search/ reruns only search.spec.ts.](../../../assets/guides/running-tasks/e2e-hash-imports.svg)

## Opt a test file in

Add the directive at the top of the test file and import the app code the test exercises:

```ts
// apps/shop-e2e/src/checkout.spec.ts
// @nx-ultracache: imports
// This test's task hashes from the app code imported below, not from the whole app.
// Keep importing the code the test exercises: https://nx.dev/docs/kb/hash-e2e-tests-from-imports
import '../../shop/src/app/checkout/checkout-page';
import { expect, test } from '@playwright/test';

test('shows the order summary', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await expect(
    page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Order summary' })
  ).toBeVisible();
});
```

Keep the two comment lines after the directive.
Nothing in the test uses the imports, so without the comment they read as dead code.

The [Playwright](/docs/technologies/test-tools/playwright/introduction) and
[Cypress](/docs/technologies/test-tools/cypress/introduction) plugins read the directive and set
`"inputs": false` on the `dependsOn` entries of the test file's atomized task that point at
servers.
Test files without the directive keep hashing the servers' inputs.
The task that runs the whole suite, `e2e` by default, and the `e2e-ci` task that groups the atomized
tasks don't change.

## Choose what to import

The task hashes what the test process reads, so the imports decide which changes rerun the test:

- Import the module of the feature the test drives, such as its page or component, rather than a
  route table or the app entry point.
  Playwright doesn't load modules the app only loads through a dynamic `import()`, such as
  lazy-loaded routes, so importing the router doesn't reach them.
- Use a bare import, `import '<path>'`, unless the test uses an imported value.
  The TypeScript transform of the test runner removes an import whose names the file doesn't use,
  and then the runner never reads that module.
- Import the feature's own files, since a barrel file that re-exports many features can make the
  task hash all of them.
- Playwright runs the imported code in Node, where a module that reads `window` or `document` when
  it loads fails the test file.

## What the hash leaves out

Without the servers' inputs, a change to app code the test process never reads doesn't rerun the
test.
The task's hash leaves out:

- Files the app references by path instead of importing, such as Angular `templateUrl` and
  `styleUrl` files, static assets, and data files the app fetches at runtime.
- App code the test exercises but doesn't import, such as the app shell, layouts, and shared
  providers.
- Other servers the task depends on, such as an API the app calls.
  Import the API code the test relies on as well.
- The servers' own configuration files, environment variables, runtime inputs, and external
  dependencies.

Don't add the directive to a test file whose results depend on any of these.

## Use the directive with Ultracache

The directive is for tasks that Nx hashes from an Ultracache recording.
The plugins apply it either way, so without a recording, such as on your machine, the task hashes
only its own declared inputs, without the servers' inputs.

## Check that the directive applies

Run `nx show project` for the e2e project:

```shell
nx show project shop-e2e --json
```

In the atomized target of an opted-in test file, the `dependsOn` entries that point at servers
have `"inputs": false`:

```json
{
  "e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts": {
    "dependsOn": [
      { "projects": ["shop"], "target": "serve", "inputs": false },
      { "target": "e2e--wait-for-webserver" }
    ]
  }
}
```

A `dependsOn` you configure for the atomized targets, in `nx.json` or in the project configuration,
replaces the inferred entries and their `"inputs": false`.
Add `"..."` to each `dependsOn` you configure for them to keep the inferred entries.

## Lint and type-check the imports

The [`@nx/enforce-module-boundaries` rule](/docs/kb/enforce-module-boundaries) reports imports of
app code from another project.
It doesn't report them in an atomized test file with the directive.

When a test file imports `.tsx` files, set `jsx` in the `compilerOptions` of the e2e project's
TypeScript configuration, such as `"jsx": "react-jsx"`, so TypeScript can check them.

## Directive syntax

- The directive goes in the comment block at the top of the file.
  It can be a line comment or sit inside a block comment, and blank lines, a `#!` line, and other
  comments can come before it.
- Nx stops reading at the first line of code, so a directive after it has no effect.
- `imports` is the only value.
  With any other value in a test file that has an atomized task, Nx fails to create the project
  graph and the error names the test file.
- The directive only applies to atomized e2e tasks that depend on a server Nx runs.
  In Playwright, that's a `webServer` that sets `reuseExistingServer` to `true` and whose
  `command` runs an Nx task.
  In Cypress, it's a `ciWebServerCommand` that runs an Nx task, unless the preset sets
  `reuseExistingServer` to `false`.
